{"conjecture": "Stepwise refinement via refinement calculus can be made fully automatable by framing each transformation as a type-theoretic morphism search: at each step, the prover enumerates candidate refinement laws (strengthening guards, introducing loops, decomposing assignments) and discharges the resulting proof obligations using SMT, accepting the first transformation whose correctness condition is provable. The key conjecture is that a fixed, ordered catalogue of approximately thirty refinement strategies—covering sequence, iteration, and assignment introduction—is complete for synthesizing executable code from any abstract specification expressible in a guarded-command language whose data types are decidable in the backing SMT theory. Completeness here means that if a refinement exists, the strategy catalogue will find one without backtracking across steps, because each refinement step monotonically reduces the nondeterminism measure of the specification."}
